## 1. Topology

**Decision:** Single Go binary (`cmd/api`), REST over Gin.
**Rationale:** One process serves all domains; a second entry point
(`cmd/worker`, `cmd/migrate`) gets its own `main.go` only once there's a real
second process — `main.go` has no importers, so adding one later touches no
other file.

## 2. Package Layout

```text
cmd/
└── api/main.go             # config, slog, graceful shutdown, wires domains
internal/
├── platform/                # talks to real external systems (DB, later: cache, queue, mail, ...)
│   └── database/
├── shared/                  # pure logic/framework glue, no I/O
│   ├── config/  apperror/  dberr/  httpx/  id/
│   └── middleware/  pagination/  tx/
└── app/                     # domain packages (one per feature)
    └── <domain>/
```

**Decision:** `platform/` vs `shared/` is split by whether the code talks to
a real external system.
**Rationale:** Code that does I/O against Postgres/cache/queue/SMTP/S3 →
`platform/`. Pure logic or HTTP-framework glue with no I/O → `shared/`. Never
add a `shared/utils` or similarly generic package — name it after what it
actually does.

## 3. API Style

**Decision:** REST over Gin, one handler package per domain, every route
grouped under{{#if apiPrefix}} `/{{apiPrefix}}`{{else}} no prefix{{/if}}.
**Rationale:** `handler → service → repository`, one direction, all three
layers in the same package — no cross-domain imports. The prefix is a
single project-wide choice made at `create` time (`--api-prefix`) — there is
no per-domain versioning; a domain that needs a real breaking change gets a
new domain package (or a new field on the existing DTO), not a duplicated
model pointed at the same table under a different URL.

## 4. Application boundaries

**Decision:** New modules use the project defaults recorded in
`go-scaffold.config.json`: surface `{{defaultModuleSurface}}` and application
style `{{defaultApplicationStyle}}`. The `generate module` wizard presents
those values through Lean/CRUD/CQRS profiles, with Advanced for an explicit
custom combination. `--profile` or the supported axis flags can override the
defaults for one module.
**Rationale:** A feature with meaningful write invariants or a different read
shape benefits from separate `CommandHandler` and `QueryHandler` ports while
remaining inside this modular monolith. A small CRUD feature does not benefit
from empty command/query wrappers, so it keeps one service until its business
needs justify the split. CQRS here does not require a second database, broker,
or event-sourcing runtime.

When enabled, `commands.go` owns state-changing application use cases,
`queries.go` owns read use cases, and `composition.go` constructs both. The
HTTP handler is still an inbound adapter and `cmd/api/wiring.go` still only
selects infrastructure and registers the feature.

## 5. Response and Error Model

**Decision:** Central `apperror.AppError` (HTTP status + machine-readable
`code` + message), rendered once by `middleware.Error()`.
**Rationale:** Handlers only ever do `c.Error(err); return` — no `c.JSON` per
call site, no error-shape drift between domains.

- Domain-specific errors live in each package's `errors.go` (e.g.
  `USER_NOT_FOUND`, not a bare `NOT_FOUND`)
- DB errors are classified once in `shared/dberr` (`IsDuplicate`,
  `IsForeignKey`) and mapped to the right HTTP status per domain

## 6. Pagination

**Decision:** Shared `limit`/`offset` parsing and response envelope
(`shared/pagination`), used by every list endpoint.
**Rationale:** One implementation, one response shape (`{data, limit,
offset}`) — no per-domain reinvention.

## 7. Persistence

**Decision:** PostgreSQL + GORM, schema managed by
[golang-migrate](https://github.com/golang-migrate/migrate).
**Rationale:** `APP_ENV=development` allows a convenience table bootstrap;
`APP_ENV=production` runs only after `migrate up` has applied the separate,
versioned, rollback-capable SQL migrations.

{{#if docker}}- `docker-compose.yml` provides the local Postgres instance
{{/if}}
## 8. IDs

**Decision:** UUID v7 for every entity, generated app-side
(`shared/id.New()`), not by a DB default.
**Rationale:** Time-ordered IDs sort by creation time, reducing B-tree page
splits versus random v4 under heavy writes — and the app has the ID before
insert, so it doesn't need `gen_random_uuid()`.

{{#if openapiDocs}}
## 9. API Documentation

**Decision:** Hand-written OpenAPI spec (`docs/openapi.yaml`), split by
domain module, read from the working copy only — the server serves no `/docs`
route in any environment. Renderers that follow relative `$ref`s get a
fully-resolved single file from `make openapi-bundle`.
**Rationale:** Cheap while the endpoint count is low; switch to
comment-generated (swaggo) if hand-updates start drifting.
{{/if}}

{{#if observability}}
## 10. Observability

**Decision:** Prometheus metrics (`GET /metrics`, request count + latency
per route) always-on when this feature is enabled; OpenTelemetry tracing
(Gin + GORM) exports via OTLP/HTTP only when `OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT`
is set — empty means no exporter is created and no network calls are made.
**Rationale:** Metrics have no external dependency to turn on (Prometheus
scrapes the app, the app never dials out) so there's no reason to gate them
further. Tracing needs a collector to be useful, so it stays off until one's
actually configured, instead of trying to dial a collector that isn't there.
This is not a one-way `create`-time door: `go-scaffold add observability`
layers the same files onto a project that started without it, the way `add
auth`/`add rbac` do — `internal/shared/middleware/{metrics,tracing}.go`,
`internal/platform/telemetry/tracing.go`, and the wiring in `cmd/api/wiring.go`
and `internal/platform/database`. Both the Gin and GORM tracing hooks are
hand-rolled against the OTel SDK directly, not the official
`otelgin`/`gorm.io/plugin/opentelemetry` contrib packages — those pull in a
newer Gin (→ HTTP/3/quic-go) and every DB driver they support tracing for
(MySQL, ClickHouse, MongoDB), respectively, for a Postgres-only project that
only wants request/query spans.

**Scope: `cmd/api` only.** `cmd/worker` is not instrumented. The GORM plugin
lives in `database.Open`, which the worker also calls, so it does raise spans —
but `telemetry.Init` is the only thing that calls `otel.SetTracerProvider` and
it runs in `cmd/api` alone, so those spans reach a no-op provider and are
discarded. The worker serves no `/metrics` either. To change that, call
`telemetry.Init` from `cmd/worker/main.go` — split it into `main`/`run` first,
the way `cmd/api` is, or the `defer` that flushes the exporter on shutdown is
skipped by the `os.Exit` calls already in there.

{{/if}}
